course: english
lesson: 55
title: Olympiad: the Spelling Bee
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: In a spelling bee the pronouncer says a word and you spell it aloud, letter by letter; one wrong letter and you are out.
explain: You may ask for the definition, the word in a sentence or the language it comes from, and the origin is a real clue: words from Greek often use "rh", "ph" and "y" (rhythm), and words from French often end in "-ette" or "-ue" (silhouette, queue).
explain: Most hard words hide a trap: a double letter (accommodate has a double c AND a double m, necessary has one c and a double s), a silent letter (rhythm, Wednesday), or a sound people say wrongly (mischievous has no "i" before -ous).
explain: Champions break a long word into chunks and use a memory trick in their head: "one collar and two sleeves" for necessary (one c, two s), "con + science" for conscience.
explain: Picture Kai from Manchester groaning "We say Wenzday, it's well tricky": the old name was "Woden's day", after a god, so the "d" and "nes" are still written although almost nobody says them.
fact: Rhythm is spelled R-H-Y-T-H-M; it comes from Greek and has no a, e, i, o or u, with "y" doing the job of a vowel.
fact: Mischievous is spelled M-I-S-C-H-I-E-V-O-U-S and said "MIS-chi-vus"; "mischievious", with an extra "i", is a very common mistake.
fact: Conscience is spelled C-O-N-S-C-I-E-N-C-E, "con" + "science", from Latin.
fact: Onomatopoeia is spelled O-N-O-M-A-T-O-P-O-E-I-A; it comes from Greek words meaning "making names", and it means a word that sounds like what it means.
fact: Silhouette is a French word spelled S-I-L-H-O-U-E-T-T-E, named after a French government minister, Étienne de Silhouette.
fact: Accommodate has a double c and a double m: A-C-C-O-M-M-O-D-A-T-E.
fact: Wednesday comes from Old English "Woden's day", which is why it keeps a "d" and "nes" that most people do not say.
fact: Fahrenheit is named after Daniel Gabriel Fahrenheit, a German-speaking scientist, and is spelled F-A-H-R-E-N-H-E-I-T, with "ei" as in German.
fact: Queue comes from French, where it means "tail", and is spelled Q-U-E-U-E: you hear only the Q, and the four letters after it add no sound.
fact: Necessary has one c and a double s: N-E-C-E-S-S-A-R-Y ("one collar, two sleeves").
fact: Pharaoh, the title of the kings of ancient Egypt, is spelled P-H-A-R-A-O-H, with the "a" before the "o".
fact: In most bees you may ask for the definition, a sentence, the language of origin and to hear the word again; you may start again, but you cannot change letters you have already said.
exercise: Your word is "rhythm". Which spelling is right? | rhythm | rythm | rhythym | rhytm
exercise: Zara asks for the language of origin of "rhythm" and hears "Greek". How can that help? | Greek words often use "rh" and "y" | Greek words never have silent letters | Greek words are always very short ones | Greek words almost always end in "-ous"
exercise: Ollie spells M-I-S-C-H-I-E-V-I-O-U-S. Is he right? | No: there is no "i" before "-ous" | Yes: that is exactly the correct spelling | No: it needs a double s after "mi" | No: it should end in "-eous" instead
exercise: How is "mischievous" said correctly? | MIS-chi-vus | mis-CHEE-vee-us | MIS-chee-VY-us | mis-CHY-vus
exercise: Priya hesitates over "conscience". Which spelling is right? | conscience | consience | conscence | consceince
exercise: Which memory trick fits "conscience"? | con + science | con + sense | cons + scene | cone + sense
exercise: Jamie's final word is "onomatopoeia". Which spelling is right? | onomatopoeia | onomatopeia | onomatapoeia | onomotopoeia
exercise: Grace asks, "Is it French?" and gets "silhouette". Which spelling is right? | silhouette | silhouete | sillhouette | silouette
exercise: Leo spells A-C-O-M-M-O-D-A-T-E and is out. What did he miss? | A second c | A second m | A second o | A final s
exercise: Which spelling follows "one collar, two sleeves"? | necessary | neccessary | necesary | neccesary
exercise: Why does "Wednesday" have a "d" that most people do not say? | It was "Woden's day" in Old English | A printer slipped it in by mistake | It was copied from the French word | The d stops it rhyming with Monday
exercise: Ellie hears that "Fahrenheit" comes from a German surname. Which spelling is right? | Fahrenheit | Farenheit | Fahrenhiet | Fahreinheit
exercise: Nadia laughs, "Queue is just a Q and four silent letters!" Which four letters follow the Q? | U, E, U, E | U, E, E, U | E, U, E, U | U, U, E, E
exercise: Josh spells N-E-C-C-E-S-S-A-R-Y. What is wrong? | One c too many | One s too few in the middle | Nothing at all is wrong | It should end in "-ery"
exercise: In the tie-break Mia writes PHARAOH and Ben writes PHAROAH. Who is right? | Mia | Ben | Both | Neither
exercise: In most bees, what are you NOT allowed to do? | Change a letter you have already said | Ask for the definition of the word | Ask which language the word is from | Ask to hear the word said again
test: Your mate says, "Asking for the origin is a waste of time, innit." Put him right. | Origins hint at spelling patterns | Origins never help with any spelling | The origin tells you the whole answer | Only the judges are told the origins
test: Kai, from Manchester: "Wenzday's just W-E-N-S-D-A-Y, sorted, our kid." What is the right spelling? | W-E-D-N-E-S-D-A-Y | W-E-N-S-D-A-Y | W-E-D-N-S-D-A-Y | W-E-N-D-S-D-A-Y
test: A cartoon speller spells "silhouete" with one t and wins the cup. Fix it. | Silhouette needs a double t | One t is right, so he deserves the cup | It needs a double l in the middle | It should have no h in it at all
test: Priyanka's dad, from Bangalore: "Do the needful, beta, spell accommodate." ("Do the needful" means do what is needed; "beta" is a fond word for a child.) Which spelling is right? | accommodate | acommodate | accomodate | acomodate
test: Jordan, rough: "Who even says 'queue', just say line, mate." In British English, what is a queue? | A line of people waiting their turn | A type of woolly hat for winter | A short tail on a cat or a dog | A crowd all shoving forwards
test: Which word has a double c AND a double m? | accommodate | necessary | mischievous | silhouette
test: Which word has one c and a double s? | necessary | accommodate | conscience | pharaoh
test: A Texan speller says, "Y'all, onomatopoeia's fixin' to break me." ("Fixin' to" means about to.) What does onomatopoeia mean? | A word that sounds like its meaning | A word spelled the same way backwards | A word with no vowels in it anywhere | Any very long word that comes from Greek
test: Which of these words has no a, e, i, o or u at all? | rhythm | queue | pharaoh | Fahrenheit
test: A sign says "Fahrenhiet". What is wrong with it? | The "ie" should be "ei" | The first h is missing | Nothing; it is right | It needs a double r
test: Which list is spelled entirely correctly? | rhythm, queue, necessary | rythm, queue, necessary | rhythm, que, necessary | rhythm, queue, neccessary
exam: Identify the correct orthography of the word denoting a regular pattern of beats. | rhythm | rythm | rhythym | rhytm
exam: Ascertain the number of instances of the letter "c" in the correctly spelled word "necessary". | One | Two | Three | None
exam: It is given that the word "Wednesday" derives from the Old English for "Woden's day". Select the response that most accurately accounts for its unpronounced letters. | The old spelling has been retained | A printer added them in error | They were borrowed from Greek | They distinguish it from Monday
exam: Determine which of the following is the correct spelling of the title of the kings of ancient Egypt. | pharaoh | pharoah | pharoh | farao
